    <node id="RADIATION THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Radiation therapy is a cancer treatment that uses high-energy waves, such as X-rays, photons, protons, and other sources of radiation, to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors. It is administered by radiation oncologists, who are experts in this treatment, and includes two main types: external beam radiation therapy and internal radiation therapy (brachytherapy). As a local treatment, it affects a specific area of the body and may be combined with chemotherapy or other systemic therapies to enhance its effect. It can be given alone, before or after surgery, as adjuvant therapy to kill remaining cancer cells, or as primary treatment when surgery is not possible or is declined. It is also used when a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery, and for unresectable cancers, sometimes with chemotherapy and systemic therapy.

Radiation therapy is used to treat many cancer types. It is a treatment option for prostate cancer, and may be avoided or delayed if molecular biomarker testing indicates lower-risk disease. For rectal cancer, it is often used and can damage the hormone-making function of the ovaries and testicles, potentially causing infertility; fertility preservation should be considered beforehand. For anal cancer, radiation therapy is almost always part of initial treatment, often combined with chemotherapy, and is given in daily fractions over 5 to 6 weeks, beginning with the first 5-FU infusion. It can also be used for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes if not previously irradiated. In anal cancer treatment, it can cause tissue damage and bowel, bladder, and sexual side effects, and may weaken or damage the perineum, affecting healing after abdominoperineal resection (APR). For cervical cancer, radiation therapy is a treatment category that includes external beam radiation therapy and brachytherapy. It is a treatment option for squamous cell skin cancer when surgery is not an option, after surgery, or with systemic therapy. It is also listed for thyroid cancer. For primary CNS lymphoma, radiation therapy is a main treatment that can slow tumor growth and reduce symptoms; whole-brain radiation therapy is the most commonly used technique. It can be delivered to the brain and sometimes expanded to include the eyes, with side effects including headaches, nausea, fatigue, and possible long-term nervous system damage. For head and neck cancers, radiation can affect endocrine glands such as the thyroid and pituitary, as well as hearing, and may require related assessments before or after treatment.

Because radiation therapy affects tissues, the same area generally cannot be retreated after an initial course of radiation.
Relationships:
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: NAUSEA
  - Description: Nausea is a common side effect of radiation therapy
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: FATIGUE
  - Description: Fatigue may begin after radiation therapy has finished and last for several weeks
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: LOSS OF APPETITE
  - Description: Loss of appetite is a common side effect of radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
