    <node id="HEART TESTS">
      <data key="d0">HEART TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">HEART TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Heart tests, also known as cardiac tests, are medical evaluations used to assess how well the heart works, measure heart function before or during treatment, and monitor potential side effects of treatments. These tests include electrocardiogram (ECG) and echocardiogram, among others. They serve to evaluate the heart's overall condition and function.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: ECHOCARDIOGRAM
  - Description: An echocardiogram is a type of heart test that uses ultrasound, or sound waves, to create pictures of the heart. This non-invasive procedure allows healthcare providers to evaluate the structure and function of the heart, providing detailed information about its chambers, valves, and overall performance. As one of the key heart tests, the echocardiogram helps in diagnosing and monitoring various cardiac conditions by visually assessing how well the heart pumps blood and how its components are working together.
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: ELECTROCARDIOGRAM
  - Description: Electrocardiogram is a heart test that shows electrical changes in the heart
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: ELECTROCARDIOGRAM (ECG OR EKG)
  - Description: An electrocardiogram is a heart test that records electrical changes, rate, and rhythm
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: CARDIAC TESTS
  - Description: Heart tests and cardiac tests are the same category of tests used to see how well the heart works
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: CARDIOLOGIST
  - Description: A patient may be referred to a cardiologist, a heart specialist, for heart or cardiac tests
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Heart tests may be used to monitor treatment side effects or measure heart function before starting treatment
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: SIDE EFFECTS
  - Description: Heart tests may be used to monitor treatment side effects

---</data>
