    <node id="FAMILY HISTORY">
      <data key="d0">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d1">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Family history is the health history of a person’s blood relatives—particularly close blood relatives—including information about cancers and other diseases such as heart disease, cancer, and diabetes. It is gathered through a process in which the care team asks about the health conditions of these relatives. Family history is used to assess inherited risk, helping clinicians identify whether a person may have a higher chance of developing certain conditions due to genetic or familial factors. In the context of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), family history is specifically used during evaluation, as having affected close blood relatives can increase a person’s risk of developing CLL. Overall, family history serves as an important tool for understanding inherited health risks and guiding care decisions.
Relationships:
  - Source: FAMILY MEMBERS
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Family members provide health history for the family history
  - Source: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Target: GENETIC TESTING
  - Description: Family history of cancer may lead to genetic testing
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Family history of cancer may make genetic testing important
  - Source: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team is responsible for collecting and reviewing information related to family history as part of patient assessments. Specifically, the care team asks about family history, gathering details about the patient's relatives and their health conditions. This process is also described as asking about the family medical history, meaning the care team solicits information regarding any medical conditions, diseases, or health patterns that may run in the patient's family. In practice, the care team's inquiry into family history encompasses the broader family medical history, ensuring that relevant hereditary and genetic factors are considered in the patient's overall care plan.
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: The care team asks about the health history of blood relatives (family history)
  - Source: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: A family history of prostate cancer means a higher chance of developing prostate cancer
  - Source: CANCER
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Some cancers can run in families, so family history is discussed.
  - Source: CLL
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: A family history of CLL makes relatives 7 to 8 times more likely to develop CLL.
  - Source: GENERAL HEALTH TESTS
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: General health tests include family history
  - Source: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Target: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history includes family history.
  - Source: MEDICAL HISTORY
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Medical history and family history are two related but distinct health histories used by the care team. Family history is a specific component of the broader medical history. While the medical history encompasses a patient’s overall health background, the family history focuses specifically on the health conditions and patterns that occur among blood relatives. Both are important for guiding clinical care, but family history contributes to the medical history by providing insight into inherited risks and familial health trends.
  - Source: GENERAL HEALTH HISTORY
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: A family history is a component of the general health information collected by the care team
  - Source: INFECTIONS
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Family history questions include illnesses, infections, and injuries.
  - Source: INJURIES
  - Target: FAMILY HISTORY
  - Description: Family history questions include illnesses, infections, and injuries.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
