    <node id="HEPATITIS B">
      <data key="d0">HEPATITIS B</data>
      <data key="d1">HEPATITIS B</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Hepatitis B is a viral infection caused by the hepatitis B virus (HBV). It is clinically significant in multiple contexts, including as a required screening test before treatment for chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), due to the risk of viral reactivation during immunosuppressive therapy. The infection can lead to serious liver-related complications, including chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, liver failure, and liver cancer. Additionally, the presence of chronic hepatitis B is an established indication for regular liver cancer screening, as it substantially increases the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEPATITIS B
  - Target: LIVER CANCER
  - Description: Hepatitis B is a risk factor for liver cancer.
  - Source: CIRRHOSIS
  - Target: HEPATITIS B
  - Description: Hepatitis B virus or infection can cause cirrhosis.
  - Source: HEPATITIS
  - Target: HEPATITIS B
  - Description: Hepatitis B is one of the viruses that cause hepatitis.
  - Source: HEPATITIS B
  - Target: CHRONIC HEPATITIS
  - Description: Hepatitis B can cause chronic hepatitis.
  - Source: HEPATITIS B
  - Target: LIVER FAILURE
  - Description: Hepatitis B can cause liver failure.
  - Source: HEPATITIS B
  - Target: HEPATITIS B AND C TEST
  - Description: The test checks for hepatitis B.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HEPATITIS C">
      <data key="d0">HEPATITIS C</data>
      <data key="d1">HEPATITIS C</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Hepatitis C is a viral infection caused by the hepatitis C virus (HCV). It is known to cause chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, liver failure, and liver cancer. In the context of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), hepatitis C is an infection that is specifically tested for before CLL treatment begins.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEPATITIS C
  - Target: LIVER CANCER
  - Description: Hepatitis C can cause liver cancer.
  - Source: CIRRHOSIS
  - Target: HEPATITIS C
  - Description: Hepatitis C virus or infection can cause cirrhosis.
