    <node id="SECONDARY LIVER CANCER">
      <data key="d0">SECONDARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d1">SECONDARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">(No description provided in the data)
Relationships:
  - Source: SECONDARY LIVER CANCER
  - Target: LIVER CANCER
  - Description: Secondary liver cancer is a form of liver cancer that occurs when cancer cells from another part of the body spread, or metastasize, to the liver. It is not a cancer that originates in the liver itself, but rather a secondary site of a primary cancer located elsewhere. As such, secondary liver cancer is categorized under the broader term "liver cancer," which encompasses both primary liver cancer (starting in the liver) and secondary liver cancer (spreading to the liver from other organs). The development of secondary liver cancer indicates that the original cancer has advanced and disseminated through the bloodstream or lymphatic system. Treatment and prognosis depend on the source of the primary cancer, the extent of liver involvement, and overall patient health.</data>
