    <node id="CANCER STAGING">
      <data key="d0">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">CANCER STAGING is the process of describing the extent of cancer in the body, including the size and location of the tumor, lymph node involvement, and whether the cancer has spread (metastasis) to distant sites. It also incorporates additional prognostic factors such as tumor grade, hormone receptor status, and HER2 status. Staging is used to guide prognosis and treatment decisions, helping clinicians determine the most appropriate therapeutic approach.

Cancer staging is performed using standardized staging systems developed by organizations such as the AJCC (American Joint Committee on Cancer) and FIGO (International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics). For ovarian cancer specifically, the FIGO system is the one most often used. These systems provide a common language for describing the extent of disease, ensuring consistency in diagnosis, treatment planning, and communication among healthcare providers.

The staging process also determines whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes or other parts of the body. For example, in anal cancer, Stage I and II cancers have not invaded nearby lymph nodes, and staging helps determine whether local excision—surgical removal of the tumor and a small margin of surrounding tissue—is appropriate. This illustrates how staging directly influences treatment options based on the precise extent of the disease.

Overall, cancer staging is a critical component of oncology practice. It involves describing the size, location, lymph node involvement, and spread of cancer, and it is essential for predicting patient outcomes, selecting treatments, and comparing results across clinical studies. By integrating multiple disease characteristics, staging provides a comprehensive picture of each patient's cancer, enabling personalized and evidence-based care.
Relationships:
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: Cancer staging describes whether cancer has metastasized to distant organs such as the lungs or liver.
  - Source: METASTASIS
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: The presence or absence of metastasis is a key factor in determining cancer stage and is represented in the M score.
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: A biopsy removes samples for testing, and the results help determine cancer stage and treatment plan.
  - Source: BIOPSY
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Biopsy results provide tissue samples that are used in cancer staging.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: NCCN provides patient education that explains cancer staging, including TNM scores and numbered stages.
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: The cancer staging system is used to guide treatment after surgery, making staging an important part of planning post-surgical care.
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Cancer staging is often performed before and after surgery, with surgery enabling more accurate pathologic staging.
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: The cancer stage determined by the staging system helps guide whether chemotherapy is recommended and which chemotherapy regimen is used.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Treatment decisions are guided by cancer stage, and treatment may begin after staging is initially determined.
  - Source: CANCER
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Cancer staging is used to reflect prognosis and guide treatment decisions for cancer.
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: IMAGING TESTS
  - Description: Imaging tests take pictures of the inside of the body and are used during cancer staging and treatment planning.
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Imaging tests provide information about tumor size, lymph node involvement, and distant spread used in cancer staging.
  - Source: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Biomarker testing is part of the information gathered during staging to identify gene mutations or proteins that may guide treatment.
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Cancer staging determines when chemoradiation is recommended, such as for high-grade stage I, stage II with sphincter invasion, and stage III cancers.
  - Source: PATHOLOGIC STAGING
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Cancer staging is the overall process of determining the extent of cancer in the body. It is often performed as pathologic staging after surgery. Pathologic staging is the post-surgery portion of cancer staging, determined from surgical tissue.
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: BREAST CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Breast cancer staging is the specific application of cancer staging to breast cancer
  - Source: AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created and developed the cancer staging system described in the text, which is the staging system used for cancer staging. This AJCC staging system is one of the two standard ways to describe the extent of ovarian cancer. As the entity responsible for this widely used framework, the American Joint Committee on Cancer established a systematic method for categorizing the progression and spread of cancer, providing clinicians and researchers with a common language to describe tumor characteristics. The cancer staging system developed by the committee is integral to treatment planning and prognosis, and its application to ovarian cancer represents one of its key clinical uses, where it serves as a standard alongside another classification approach to define how far the disease has advanced.
  - Source: CLINICAL STAGING
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Clinical staging is the pre-treatment portion of cancer staging. Cancer staging is often performed as clinical staging before treatment begins.
  - Source: INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics developed the FIGO staging system, which is the most commonly used system for describing ovarian cancer extent.
  - Source: LOCAL EXCISION
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Cancer staging determines whether local excision is an option, since it is recommended only for certain stage I and IIA cancers.
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: TNM SCORES
  - Description: Cancer staging uses TNM scores for tumor, node, and metastasis.
  - Source: CANCER STAGING
  - Target: NASOPHARYNGEAL CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Nasopharyngeal cancer staging is a specific type of cancer staging.
  - Source: TNM SCORING
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: TNM scores are assigned as part of cancer staging and are then combined to assign a numbered stage.</data>
