    <node id="CLINICAL TRIAL">
      <data key="d0">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d1">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A clinical trial is a type of medical research study in which potential new cancer treatments and new ways of fighting cancer are studied in people after laboratory development and testing. These trials are often conducted in phases, and if a treatment is found to be safe and effective, a successful trial may lead to approval by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Clinical trials use investigational treatments that may, in time, become part of standard care; indeed, current standards of care are based on results from past clinical trials.

Patients may enroll in clinical trials, and the study sponsor usually pays for research-related costs. Depending on the trial, participants may receive an investigational treatment, may continue receiving standard cancer care during the trial, or may receive both. A patient’s performance status may determine eligibility, and for some cancers, biomarker testing results may help determine whether a patient can enroll—for example, in the setting of recurrent cervical cancer. Patients may be able to join a clinical trial at any time during their care.

Clinical trials are recommended or encouraged as treatment options across several cancer types. For primary CNS lymphoma, participating in a clinical trial is a recommended treatment option, and it may be an option for induction treatment while helping researchers learn more about the disease. People with anal cancer, including metastatic anal cancer, are encouraged to consider enrolling in a trial if one is available. Patients with persistent or recurrent ovarian cancer are also encouraged to enroll. For gliomas, clinical trials test potential new treatments, and the number of such trials has increased in recent years, offering hope to people living with this disease. In bladder cancer, clinical trials may be used as research treatment options when intravesical BCG does not work or when regular BCG treatment is unavailable.

Additionally, clinical trials are referenced as the recommended setting for ctDNA testing, which looks for small pieces of DNA released by tumor cells into the blood. Patients can search for available trials through various organizations. Overall, a clinical trial is a research study that offers patients access to investigational therapies while contributing to medical knowledge and the development of future cancer treatments.
Relationships:
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: TREATMENT TRIAL
  - Description: Clinical trials are research studies that evaluate medical interventions in human participants. Treatment trials are a specific type of clinical trial, meaning that all treatment trials fall under the broader category of clinical trials. In the context of cancer, treatment trials focus specifically on cancer treatments—testing new therapies, drug combinations, surgical techniques, or other interventions to determine their safety and effectiveness. Because most cancer clinical trials are treatment trials, treatment trials constitute a major subset of clinical research in oncology. Thus, while every treatment trial is a clinical trial, not every clinical trial is a treatment trial; clinical trials also include prevention, screening, diagnostic, and quality-of-life studies. In summary, treatment trials are a type of clinical trial that centers on cancer treatments and represents the majority of cancer-related clinical research.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: NCCN
  - Description: NCCN Cancer Centers and NCCN patient guidelines provide information about cancer clinical trials and treatment.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Enrolling in a clinical trial is another treatment option alongside surgery for eligible patients.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The care team plays a key role in evaluating whether a clinical trial is appropriate for a patient. They can be consulted directly to determine if a clinical trial might be right for the individual. To make this determination, the care team uses the patient's performance status as a key factor in assessing eligibility for a clinical trial.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: A clinical trial is a treatment option that may be considered for a patient. Clinical trials offer potential new therapies and serve as another treatment option.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: UNITED STATES
  - Description: The text describes finding clinical trials in the United States.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
  - Description: NCI provides a portal for patients to search for cancer clinical trials.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE
  - Description: NLM maintains clinicaltrials.gov, where patients can search for clinical trials worldwide.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: NCCN CANCER CENTERS
  - Description: The NCCN Cancer Centers serve as a key resource for locating clinical trials, specifically within the United States. These centers assist patients in identifying and accessing clinical trial opportunities, making them a valuable source of information for those seeking participation in research studies. The entity CLINICAL TRIAL is central to this resource, as the primary function of the NCCN Cancer Centers is to connect individuals with relevant clinical trials. In summary, NCCN Cancer Centers provide a United States–based resource dedicated to helping patients find clinical trials.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: PHASE 3 TRIALS
  - Description: Phase 3 trials are a phase of clinical trials comparing a new approach to a standard treatment.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: PHASE 2 TRIALS
  - Description: Phase 2 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying effectiveness against a specific type of cancer.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: PHASE 4 TRIALS
  - Description: Phase 4 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying long-term safety and benefit after FDA approval.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: PHASE 1 TRIALS
  - Description: Phase 1 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying safety and side effects.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: PHASE 3 CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: A Phase 3 trial is a clinical trial phase that compares a new treatment to a standard treatment and may lead to FDA approval.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: PHASE 4 CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: A Phase 4 trial is a post-approval clinical trial phase that studies long-term safety and benefit.</data>
    </node>
