    <node id="CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE">
      <data key="d0">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
      <data key="d1">CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">The Cancer Information Service (CIS) is a service of the National Cancer Institute (NCI), part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health. It serves as NCI's comprehensive cancer information and clinical trial referral service, helping people find and learn about clinical trials while also answering cancer-related questions. The CIS can be reached by phone at 1-800-4-CANCER (1-800-422-6237) and through the NCI website at cancer.gov/contact. As an integral part of the National Cancer Institute, the CIS provides accurate, up-to-date cancer information and assists individuals in locating and understanding clinical trial options.
Relationships:
  - None explicitly listed in the provided relationship data

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CANCER INFORMATION SERVICE
Description: The Cancer Information Service (CIS) is a service of the National Cancer Institute (NCI), part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health. It serves as NCI's comprehensive cancer information and clinical trial referral service, helping people find and learn about clinical trials while also answering cancer-related questions. The CIS can be reached by phone at 1-800-4-CANCER (1-800-422-6237) and through the NCI website at cancer.gov/contact. As an integral part of the National Cancer Institute, the CIS provides accurate, up-to-date cancer information and assists individuals in locating and understanding clinical trial options.
Relationships:
  - None explicitly listed in the provided relationship data

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DISTRESS SCREENING">
      <data key="d0">DISTRESS SCREENING</data>
      <data key="d1">DISTRESS SCREENING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Distress screening is a structured assessment used to identify emotional distress in cancer patients as part of their comprehensive cancer care. It evaluates a patient's level of mental, physical, social, or spiritual distress, including depression, anxiety, and sleep issues. By systematically assessing these domains, distress screening helps care teams recognize and address the multifaceted challenges that can accompany a cancer diagnosis and treatment.
Relationships:
  - None explicitly listed in the provided relationship data

---</data>
