    <node id="HEALTH CHECKUP">
      <data key="d0">HEALTH CHECKUP</data>
      <data key="d1">HEALTH CHECKUP</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">HEALTH CHECKUP is a medical examination performed to evaluate a patient’s health status. In the context of post-treatment care, it is used to assess a patient’s recovery, blood counts, and overall condition after stem cell rescue treatment. Additionally, a health checkup may refer to a regular medical visit during which preventive services such as breast cancer screening and consultations with health care providers can take place. These two roles are complementary, reflecting both focused follow-up after a specific procedure and routine preventive care.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-RAY">
      <data key="d0">X-RAY</data>
      <data key="d1">X-RAY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">X-ray is a form of medical imaging that uses small amounts of radiation to create pictures of the inside of the body, also known as a radiograph. As an imaging technique, it relies on radiation to visualize internal structures and is commonly employed in a variety of examinations, including CT scans, chest imaging, and pyelograms. A CT scan is described as a more detailed kind of x-ray, providing greater cross-sectional detail than a standard x-ray. A chest x-ray is a specific x-ray examination focused on the chest area. While x-rays use radiation, other imaging modalities such as MRI do not use radiation. Overall, x-ray is a foundational imaging method that uses controlled, low-dose radiation for diagnostic purposes, with CT as a more advanced application and chest x-ray as a targeted exam.
Relationships: 9</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: X-RAY
Description: X-ray is a form of medical imaging that uses small amounts of radiation to create pictures of the inside of the body, also known as a radiograph. As an imaging technique, it relies on radiation to visualize internal structures and is commonly employed in a variety of examinations, including CT scans, chest imaging, and pyelograms. A CT scan is described as a more detailed kind of x-ray, providing greater cross-sectional detail than a standard x-ray. A chest x-ray is a specific x-ray examination focused on the chest area. While x-rays use radiation, other imaging modalities such as MRI do not use radiation. Overall, x-ray is a foundational imaging method that uses controlled, low-dose radiation for diagnostic purposes, with CT as a more advanced application and chest x-ray as a targeted exam.
Relationships: 9</data>
    </node>
