    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging modality that merges positron emission tomography (PET) with computed tomography (CT). It uses the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), also known as F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose, to detect cancer by showing glucose uptake in cells. Patients must not eat or drink for at least 4 hours before the scan. This imaging test is used to evaluate cancer and can identify malignant involvement in primary sites, lymph nodes, and/or distant sites. It is particularly useful when other imaging results are unclear, and the scan can be performed at the same time as a diagnostic CT. In specific clinical contexts, FDG-PET/CT is used as a follow-up imaging scan from the neck to the midthigh, usually performed 3 to 6 months after treatment for certain cervical cancer stages. Additionally, it may be used when leukemia outside the blood and bone marrow is suspected. The combination of PET and CT provides both metabolic and anatomic information, aiding in the detection and evaluation of cancer.
Relationships:
  - None listed in the provided data.

---</data>
    </node>
