    <node id="T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA">
      <data key="d0">T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d1">T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that starts in T-cell lymphocytes. It can cause an enlarged thymus, which may lead to breathing problems. When T-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in the thymus instead of the bone marrow, it is called T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: T-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.
  - Source: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: ALL
  - Description: T-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL.
  - Source: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: T-ALL
  - Description: T-ALL is the acronym for T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia.
  - Source: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: B-cell ALL is more common than T-cell ALL.
  - Source: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: T-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA
  - Description: T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma occurs when T-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in the thymus instead of the bone marrow, a presentation of T-ALL. When T-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in the thymus instead of the bone marrow, T-ALL is called T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.

---</data>
