ENTITY: RECURRENCE
Description: Recurrence, also called relapse, is the return of cancer after a period of being cancer-free following treatment. It is a key concern across many cancer types, and its likelihood and characteristics are considered during risk assessment and treatment planning. Doctors evaluate the risk of recurrence to determine whether chemotherapy is needed, and surveillance is conducted to detect it as early as possible. Detection commonly involves imaging such as MRI and PSMA-PET scans, as well as blood tests and biomarker testing. Recurrence may be persistent, local/regional, or distant, and the exact pattern influences treatment decisions—for example, treatment depends on whether cancer returns to the original site, nearby lymph nodes, or distant areas.

Recurrence is described specifically for several cancers: it is the return of bladder cancer after treatment; the return of cervical cancer after treatment, which may be persistent, local/regional, or distant and is investigated with imaging, blood tests, and biomarker testing; the return of basal cell skin cancer to the original area or its spread; and the possible return of prostate cancer after treatment. For cancers such as anal cancer, treatment depends on whether the recurrence appears in the anus, nearby lymph nodes, or distant sites. In some cancers, recurrence status determines whether the disease is considered platinum-resistant or platinum-sensitive, which directly guides subsequent treatment. Overall, recurrence represents the reappearance of cancer after initial treatment, and its management is tailored to the specific cancer type, location, extent, and biological behavior.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: TARGETED THERAPIES
Description: Treatments for which biomarker testing may determine eligibility
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: BRAF V600E MUTATION
