    <node id="CLINICAL TRIALS">
      <data key="d0">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d1">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Clinical trials are medical research studies that evaluate new treatment methods and investigational cancer treatments in people. They test the safety and effectiveness of drugs, devices, and treatment approaches, often through structured, phased research studies. If a treatment is found safe and effective in a clinical trial, it may in time be approved by the U.S. FDA. Clinical trials are listed as treatment options for several cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer, thyroid cancer, ovarian cancer, acute myeloid leukemia (AML), and cervical cancer. In colorectal cancer, specific clinical trials are evaluating the usefulness of ctDNA testing in predicting whether the cancer is likely to return. For AML, clinical trials are research treatment studies that may offer another treatment option, and patients can ask their care team if a trial might be appropriate. Overall, clinical trials provide access to investigational treatments, help bring new therapies to patients, and advance treatment for everyone; patients can ask their care team about access to them.
Relationships:
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: NCCN
  - Description: NCCN provides a clinical trial finding resource at NCCN.org/cancercenters.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The clinical trials process is closely coordinated with the patient’s care team. Patients are advised to ask their care team about access to clinical trials, and the care team plays a central role in facilitating clinical trial participation. In this capacity, the care team also advises the patient on whether a clinical trial might be right for them, helping to guide the decision-making process. Together, these responsibilities ensure that patients receive personalized support and clear information when considering clinical trial options.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Clinical trials are listed as a treatment option for cervical cancer.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Description: Systemic therapy and clinical trials are both treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SMOKING">
      <data key="d0">SMOKING</data>
      <data key="d1">SMOKING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Smoking is a behavior involving the use of tobacco, and it is recognized as a significant lifestyle-related factor that increases the risk of several types of cancer, including anal cancer and bladder cancer. Beyond its role in cancer development, smoking—or having a history of smoking—also negatively affects cancer treatment outcomes. It can make cancer treatment less effective and increases the risk of surgical side effects. However, quitting smoking can lead to better cancer treatment outcomes, highlighting the importance of smoking cessation for improved prognosis and overall health.
Relationships:
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Description: The text advises people who smoke to seek help to quit
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: Quitting smoking is the act of stopping smoking
  - Source: SMOKEFREE.GOV
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: SmokeFree.gov is designed for people who smoke and want support quitting
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: Smoking and vaping are both routes of nicotine use and are discussed together in the text. The text groups smoking and vaping together as behaviors to quit because they can affect cancer treatment.
  - Source: BETOBACCOFREE.GOV
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: BeTobaccoFree.gov is a tobacco cessation resource related to smoking
  - Source: CDC.GOV/TOBACCO
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: CDC.gov/tobacco provides information about smoking and tobacco use
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for most people who smoke.
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Smoking can limit how well cancer treatment works
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Smoking increases the chances of side effects during and after surgery
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: Quitting smoking can lead to better cervical cancer treatment outcomes, so smoking is related to cervical cancer management
  - Source: CANCER
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: Smoking increases the risk of developing cancer
  - Source: BLADDER CANCER
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: Smoking increases the risk of bladder cancer and makes death from bladder cancer more likely
  - Source: ANAL CANCER
  - Target: SMOKING
  - Description: Smoking is listed as a risk factor for anal cancer

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QUITTING SMOKING">
      <data key="d0">QUITTING SMOKING</data>
      <data key="d1">QUITTING SMOKING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Quitting smoking is the process of stopping the use of tobacco cigarettes and other smoked products. It is a significant health intervention that may reduce cancer risk and improve outcomes for cancer treatment, particularly for bladder cancer. The act of quitting smoking is supported by a range of resources, including counseling, medications, and online tools, all of which can help individuals succeed in their efforts. By discontinuing smoking, individuals can experience better cancer treatment results and lower their overall risk of developing cancer. This comprehensive approach highlights both the health benefits and the practical support available for those seeking to quit.
Relationships:
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Description: The text advises people who smoke to seek help to quit
  - Source: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Target: SMOKING
