    <node id="HEARING TEST">
      <data key="d0">HEARING TEST</data>
      <data key="d1">HEARING TEST</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">HEARING TEST is a diagnostic procedure used to determine whether hearing loss is present, as well as its type, severity, and possible underlying cause. The results of a pure-tone hearing test are typically recorded on an audiogram, which graphically displays an individual’s hearing thresholds across different frequencies. This test is particularly relevant in the context of head and neck tumors, because both the tumors themselves and their treatments (such as surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy) can adversely affect hearing. For this reason, a hearing test—often in the form of an audiogram or comprehensive hearing assessment—is commonly incorporated as part of treatment planning for affected patients. In summary, the hearing test serves both diagnostic and pre-treatment evaluative purposes, helping to identify existing hearing issues and to establish a baseline for monitoring any changes due to disease or therapy.
Relationships:
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: HEARING TEST
  - Description: Treatment of head and neck tumors can affect hearing, so a hearing test may be used
  - Source: PATIENT
  - Target: HEARING TEST
  - Description: A hearing test can assess hearing loss that may be caused by tumors or treatment
  - Source: HEARING TEST
  - Target: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: Treatment of head and neck tumors, including radiation therapy, can affect hearing
  - Source: HEARING TEST
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Hearing tests are relevant because treatment of head and neck tumors can affect hearing.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: HEARING TEST
  - Description: Tumors in the head and neck and their treatment can affect hearing, making hearing tests necessary
  - Source: TREATMENT PLANNING
  - Target: HEARING TEST
  - Description: Hearing test (audiogram) is an as-needed part of treatment planning

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Preventing pregnancy during treatment refers to the measures taken to avoid pregnancy while undergoing cancer treatment, and it is an important topic in cancer care. This practice is essential because cancer and its treatment can affect the ovaries and damage sperm, potentially impacting fertility and the health of a future pregnancy. The available options for preventing pregnancy during this time include hormonal birth control, intrauterine devices (IUDs), and barrier methods. These measures are discussed with patients to help them avoid pregnancy during treatment, as the therapies may be harmful to a developing fetus and to the individual's reproductive health. By addressing this topic, healthcare providers support patients in making informed decisions about their reproductive health and family planning during a critical period of their medical care.
Relationships:
  - Source: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Target: PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT
  - Description: Preventing pregnancy during treatment is important because cancer treatment can damage sperm and ovaries.
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER
  - Target: PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT
  - Description: Cancer and cancer treatment for head and neck cancer can affect ovaries and damage sperm, so preventing pregnancy during treatment is important
  - Source: TESTING FOR NPC
  - Target: PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT
  - Description: Preventing pregnancy during treatment is a topic covered in the testing chapter before treatment
  - Source: PREGNANCY TEST
  - Target: PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT
  - Description: A pregnancy test is done before starting treatment, and birth control is recommended during and after treatment to prevent pregnancy</data>
    </node>
