    <node id="PREGNANCY TEST">
      <data key="d0">PREGNANCY TEST</data>
      <data key="d1">PREGNANCY TEST</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A pregnancy test is a medical screening performed on individuals who can become pregnant, prior to the initiation of treatment. Specifically, it is given before cancer treatment begins, because cancer therapies can harm a developing baby. The purpose of the test is to determine whether the patient is pregnant at that time, so that appropriate precautions or adjustments to treatment can be made. This test is a standard pre-treatment step to protect both the patient and any potential fetus from exposure to harmful cancer treatments.  
Relationships:  
  - Source: PREGNANCY  
  - Target: PREGNANCY TEST  
  - Description: A pregnancy test detects pregnancy.  
  - Source: PREGNANCY TEST  
  - Target: CANCER CARE TEAM  
  - Description: The cancer care team gives a pregnancy test before treatment.  
  - Source: PATIENT  
  - Target: PREGNANCY TEST  
  - Description: Those who can become pregnant will have a pregnancy test before starting treatment  
  - Source: PREGNANCY TEST  
  - Target: PREVENTING PREGNANCY DURING TREATMENT  
  - Description: A pregnancy test is done before starting treatment, and birth control is recommended during and after treatment to prevent pregnancy  
  - Source: PREGNANCY TEST  
  - Target: BLOOD TESTS  
  - Description: The pregnancy test is listed in the blood-test section and is recommended before treatment begins.  
  - Source: HEAD AND NECK CANCER  
  - Target: PREGNANCY TEST  
  - Description: Those with head and neck cancer who can become pregnant will have a pregnancy test before starting treatment</data>
    </node>
