Description: People carrying certain inherited genetic mutations; identified as being at greater risk for prostate cancer.
Relationships:
  - Source: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Target: PEOPLE WITH INHERITED GENETIC MUTATIONS
  - Description: People with certain inherited genetic mutations are at greater risk for prostate cancer.

ENTITY: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation is a tumor characteristic defined by the presence of high microsatellite instability (MSI-H) or deficient mismatch repair (dMMR). This mutation status occurs when errors or defects in the DNA mismatch repair system are present and are not corrected, leading to the accumulation of mutations, particularly in microsatellite regions of the genome. As a result, tumors with MSI-H/dMMR exhibit a distinct genetic profile that is clinically significant. This mutation status is relevant to biomarker testing, as it helps identify patients who may respond to certain treatments. Specifically, pembrolizumab, an immune checkpoint inhibitor, is often used to treat tumors that are MSI-H or dMMR, regardless of their location in the body. In summary, MSI-H/dMMR mutation represents a key predictive biomarker in oncology, guiding treatment decisions and enabling targeted immunotherapy for affected patients.
Relationships:
  - Source: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)
  - Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation includes deficient mismatch repair.
  - Source: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)
  - Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Description: MSI-H is high microsatellite instability, often due to dMMR genes, and is part of the MSI-H/dMMR tumor mutation status.
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation is a type of mutation
  - Source: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Description: Biomarker testing can identify MSI-H/dMMR mutation status
  - Source: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Target: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY
  - Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation is a type of DNA change that leads to microsatellite instability-high or deficient mismatch repair. Microsatellite instability is a characteristic feature of MSI-H/dMMR mutation. In other words, the MSI-H/dMMR mutation drives the development of microsatellite instability, making the two closely related in the context of genomic alterations.
  - Source: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Target: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR
  - Description: Deficient mismatch repair is a cause or component of MSI-H/dMMR mutation
  - Source: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Target: MSI-H
  - Description: MSI-H is a form of MSI-H/dMMR mutation
  - Source: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Target: TMB-H
  - Description: Both TMB-H and MSI-H/dMMR are tumor mutation statuses that may guide treatment with immunotherapy drugs.

ENTITY: GERMLINE MUTATION
Description: A germline mutation is a gene mutation inherited from a birth parent. It is an inherited mutation present in every cell of the body and is passed down in families, which is why it is also called a hereditary mutation. Germline mutations can increase an individual's cancer risk, can be passed on to children, and may also be present in blood relatives.
Relationships:
  - Source: GERMLINE TESTING
  - Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Description: Germline testing looks for germline mutations in every cell.
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Target: HEREDITARY GENE TESTING
  - Description: Hereditary gene testing looks for inherited germline mutations in every cell
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Germline mutation is a type of mutation that is inherited and present in every cell
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Description: A germline mutation is an inherited type of mutation.
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Target: SOMATIC MUTATION
  - Description: Germline and somatic mutations are the two described categories of mutations; one is inherited, the other acquired
  - Source: GENETIC CANCER RISK TESTING
  - Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Description: Genetic cancer risk testing looks for germline mutations inherited from birth parents.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
  - Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
  - Description: Genetic testing can detect germline mutations relevant to cancer risk.

ENTITY: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
Description: Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary condition and an inherited syndrome that significantly increases the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. It is also closely related to colon cancer, as it is an inherited syndrome that causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum. These polyps can eventually lead to invasive colon cancer if left untreated. Thus, FAP is a genetic disorder characterized by the development of numerous colorectal polyps and an elevated risk for both adrenal tumors and invasive colorectal cancer.
Relationships:
  - Source: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
  - Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
  - Description: Familial adenomatous polyposis is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
  - Description: Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition.
  - Source: POLYPS
  - Target: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
  - Description: FAP causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum

