    <node id="POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)">
      <data key="d0">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)</data>
      <data key="d1">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET) is a medical imaging technique that uses a sugar-based radiotracer to detect cancer cells and assess the spread of cancer. It may be performed alone or combined with computed tomography (CT) or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) for more detailed diagnostic information.
Relationships:
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Target: PET/CT
  - Description: PET is the emission-imaging component of PET/CT; PET/CT combines PET with CT.
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Description: Positron emission tomography (PET) is a type of imaging technology and a specific imaging test that falls under the broader category of imaging. It is a medical diagnostic procedure that relies on radiotracers—radioactive substances introduced into the body—to detect and visualize cancer activity. By tracking how these radiotracers behave, PET imaging provides detailed information about metabolic and physiological processes, making it particularly useful for identifying malignant tumors, evaluating how far cancer has spread, and monitoring the effectiveness of cancer treatments. The entity "imaging" serves as the overarching field, while "positron emission tomography" is a specialized modality within that field, distinguished by its use of radiotracers to target and highlight areas of abnormal cellular activity, especially in oncology.
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: PET may be combined with CT or MRI to detect cancer spread.
  - Source: MRI
  - Target: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Description: PET can be combined with MRI for imaging.
  - Source: COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Description: PET may be performed with CT in a combined PET/CT scan.
  - Source: COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (CT)
  - Target: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Description: PET may be combined with CT.
  - Source: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING (MRI)
  - Target: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)
  - Description: PET may be combined with MRI.
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: PET may be performed with MRI instead of CT in some cases.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
