    <node id="POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY">
      <data key="d0">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY</data>
      <data key="d1">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Positron emission tomography (PET) is an imaging test, also referred to simply as PET, and is categorized among the various types of medical imaging. This diagnostic technique utilizes small amounts of radioactive materials known as radiotracers to produce images of the body. PET is particularly effective at highlighting areas where cells are using sugar more quickly than normal, with a special emphasis on detecting regions of heightened metabolic activity, such as those found in cancer cells. By tracking this increased sugar consumption, PET imaging helps visualize and identify the presence and extent of disease within the body.
Relationships:
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: PET may be performed with MRI instead of CT in some cases.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY
Description: Positron emission tomography (PET) is an imaging test, also referred to simply as PET, and is categorized among the various types of medical imaging. This diagnostic technique utilizes small amounts of radioactive materials known as radiotracers to produce images of the body. PET is particularly effective at highlighting areas where cells are using sugar more quickly than normal, with a special emphasis on detecting regions of heightened metabolic activity, such as those found in cancer cells. By tracking this increased sugar consumption, PET imaging helps visualize and identify the presence and extent of disease within the body.
Relationships:
  - Source: POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY
  - Target: MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
  - Description: PET may be performed with MRI instead of CT in some cases.

---</data>
