    <node id="PET/CT SCAN">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d1">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">PET/CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with a computed tomography (CT) scan into a single examination. This combined imaging technique integrates PET and CT to provide both metabolic information and anatomical detail in one scan, producing more comprehensive and detailed cross-sectional images than either method could achieve alone.

The PET portion of the scan detects metabolic activity, showing how tissues and organs are functioning at a cellular level, while the CT component provides precise anatomical structure and localization. By fusing these two types of data, the PET/CT scan allows physicians to pinpoint areas of abnormal metabolic activity within their exact anatomical context. This makes it particularly valuable for oncology, where it is used to detect, characterize, and monitor various cancers. For example, a combined PET and CT scan can create detailed images to find cancer that has spread outside the bladder, and it can also identify cancer in the prostate as well as its spread to a pelvic lymph node. In this way, the procedure helps in staging cancers and guiding treatment decisions by revealing both the location and the metabolic activity of malignant lesions. The result is a more detailed and accurate image than a PET scan or CT scan performed separately, making PET/CT a powerful tool in modern medical diagnostics.
Relationships:
  - None listed in the provided data.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: PET/CT SCAN
Description: PET/CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that combines a positron emission tomography (PET) scan with a computed tomography (CT) scan into a single examination. This combined imaging technique integrates PET and CT to provide both metabolic information and anatomical detail in one scan, producing more comprehensive and detailed cross-sectional images than either method could achieve alone.

The PET portion of the scan detects metabolic activity, showing how tissues and organs are functioning at a cellular level, while the CT component provides precise anatomical structure and localization. By fusing these two types of data, the PET/CT scan allows physicians to pinpoint areas of abnormal metabolic activity within their exact anatomical context. This makes it particularly valuable for oncology, where it is used to detect, characterize, and monitor various cancers. For example, a combined PET and CT scan can create detailed images to find cancer that has spread outside the bladder, and it can also identify cancer in the prostate as well as its spread to a pelvic lymph node. In this way, the procedure helps in staging cancers and guiding treatment decisions by revealing both the location and the metabolic activity of malignant lesions. The result is a more detailed and accurate image than a PET scan or CT scan performed separately, making PET/CT a powerful tool in modern medical diagnostics.
Relationships:
