    <node id="DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d1">DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Direct laryngoscopy is a detailed diagnostic procedure performed under general anesthesia in an operating room. Using a laryngoscope (a specialized scope) placed in the back of the throat, the doctor is able to examine deeper areas of the throat than is possible with a standard laryngoscopy. This technique allows the physician to visualize the throat thoroughly, take a tissue sample for biopsy, identify the primary tumor, and determine whether the tumor is resectable (surgically removable).
Relationships:
  - Source: DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Direct laryngoscopy allows the doctor to take a tissue sample for biopsy
  - Source: DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Direct laryngoscopy is used to determine if a tumor can be removed with surgery
  - Source: LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Target: DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Description: Laryngoscopy is a medical procedure used to visualize the inside of the larynx (voice box) and nearby structures of the throat. Direct laryngoscopy is a specific type of laryngoscopy. In direct laryngoscopy, the practitioner inserts a rigid or flexible scope directly into the airway to obtain a clear view of the larynx, often for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes. Both descriptions confirm that direct laryngoscopy falls under the broader category of laryngoscopy, making it one of the methods within this diagnostic and interventional technique.
  - Source: SCOPING PROCEDURES
  - Target: DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Description: Direct laryngoscopy uses a scope under anesthesia, making it a scoping procedure.
  - Source: DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Target: GENERAL ANESTHESIA
  - Description: Direct laryngoscopy is performed under general anesthesia
  - Source: FIBEROPTIC LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Target: DIRECT LARYNGOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are laryngoscopy procedures; fiberoptic laryngoscopy is quicker and less invasive, while direct laryngoscopy is a more detailed exam under anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
