ENTITY: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
Description: B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that develops in B-cell lymphocytes. It is more common than T-ALL. When B-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in the lymph nodes instead of the bone marrow, it is called B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.

ENTITY: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
Description: T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that starts in T-cell lymphocytes. It can cause an enlarged thymus, which may lead to breathing problems. When T-cell lymphoblasts grow mostly in the thymus instead of the bone marrow, it is called T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: T-CELL ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Description: T-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.

ENTITY: B-ALL
Description: B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that arises from B-cell lymphocytes. It is one of the two main types of ALL, the other being T-ALL (T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia), and it is notably more common than T-ALL. B-ALL encompasses several distinct subtypes, including mature B-cell ALL and forms defined by genetic markers, such as BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL (also known as Philadelphia chromosome-positive ALL) and BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. As a disease of B-cell precursors, B-ALL is characterized by the malignant proliferation of immature B cells in the bone marrow, which can interfere with normal blood cell production. Overall, B-ALL represents a major category of childhood and adult leukemia, and its classification into genetic subtypes is important for prognosis and treatment planning.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.

ENTITY: T-ALL
Description: T-ALL, the acronym for T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that originates in T-cell lymphocytes. As one of the two main types of ALL, it is less common than B-ALL. This condition may cause an enlarged thymus. Treatment options for T-ALL include participation in a clinical trial or chemotherapy.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: T-ALL
  - Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts. One of the two main types of ALL is T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). T-ALL specifically originates in T-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell that plays a central role in the immune system. As one of the two primary subtypes of ALL, T-ALL is distinguished from the other main type, which arises from B-cell lymphocytes. Together, these two subtypes encompass the majority of acute lymphoblastic leukemia cases. T-ALL develops when genetic mutations or other alterations cause T-cell precursors to multiply uncontrollably, leading to an accumulation of abnormal cells in the bone marrow, blood, and sometimes other organs. The disease is aggressive and requires prompt treatment, typically involving chemotherapy, and in some cases targeted therapy or stem cell transplantation. Although T-ALL and B-ALL share many clinical features, they differ in their cellular origins, immunophenotypic markers, and certain molecular characteristics, which can influence treatment response and prognosis. T-ALL is more common in adolescents and young adults, while B-ALL is more frequent in younger children. Because T-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, all general information about ALL—including its causes, symptoms such as fatigue, fever, easy bruising, and bone pain, as well as diagnostic approaches like blood counts and bone marrow biopsy—also applies to T-ALL. However, specific management strategies for T-ALL may be adapted based on the distinct biology of T-cell disease. In summary, T-ALL is one of the two main forms of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, originating specifically from T-cell lymphocytes, and is a distinct yet closely related entity within the broader ALL classification.

ENTITY: BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL
Description: BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) characterized by the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene. This gene produces the BCR::ABL protein, which drives the disease. Treatment for this subtype specifically aims to stop the activity of the BCR::ABL protein and typically involves an intensive combination of systemic therapies.
Relationships:
  - None listed in the provided data.

ENTITY: BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL
Description: BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL is a subtype of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). It is recognized as the most common type of B-ALL. For this subtype, participation in a clinical trial is considered the preferred treatment approach.
Relationships:
  - None listed in the provided data.

