    <node id="LYMPH NODE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">LYMPH NODE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">LYMPH NODE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Lymph node biopsy is a diagnostic procedure in which a sample of tissue is removed from a lymph node for laboratory testing. It is commonly performed when cancer spread to the lymph nodes is suspected, allowing pathologists to determine whether malignant cells are present. The procedure may be guided by imaging techniques such as ultrasound or CT scan to ensure accurate targeting of the affected node. Additionally, lymph node biopsy may be utilized when the BPDCN subtype of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is suspected, as it provides essential tissue for confirming the diagnosis. Overall, this biopsy is a key tool for detecting cancer involvement and supporting diagnosis in specific hematologic conditions.
Relationships:
  - Source: BIOPSY
  - Target: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
  - Description: Lymph node biopsy is a type of biopsy
  - Source: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
  - Target: SKIN LESION BIOPSY
  - Description: Both lymph node biopsy and skin lesion biopsy may be performed if the BPDCN subtype of AML is suspected
  - Source: CT SCAN
  - Target: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
  - Description: CT scan may be used to guide a lymph node biopsy
  - Source: ULTRASOUND
  - Target: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
  - Description: Ultrasound may be used to guide a lymph node biopsy
  - Source: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY
  - Target: NEEDLE BIOPSY
  - Description: A lymph node biopsy is performed using a needle biopsy to take a sample from the lymph node</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENTITY: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)">
      <data key="d0">ENTITY: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">ENTITY: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A defect in mismatch repair proteins that prevents DNA errors from being fixed, often leading to microsatellite instability.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">
Description: A defect in mismatch repair proteins that prevents DNA errors from being fixed, often leading to microsatellite instability.
Relationships:
  - Source: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)
  - Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation includes deficient mismatch repair.
  - Source: MUTATION
  - Target: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR
  - Description: Deficient mismatch repair arises when DNA mutations prevent mismatch repair proteins from fixing errors.
  - Source: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
  - Target: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR
  - Description: Deficient mismatch repair is a cause or component of MSI-H/dMMR mutation
  - Source: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)
  - Target: DEFICIENT MISMATCH REPAIR (DMMR)
  - Description: Deficient mismatch repair prevents DNA errors from being fixed, leading to microsatellite instability.

**ENTITY: BIOMARKER TUMOR TESTING**
Description: Biomarker tumor testing is another name for biomarker testing and includes tests of genes or their products to identify mutations or proteins that guide treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: BIOMARKER TESTING
  - Target: BIOMARKER TUMOR TESTING
  - Description: Biomarker tumor testing is another term for biomarker testing

**ENTITY: TUMOR TESTING**
Description: Also called somatic testing, molecular tumor testing, or tumor profiling; analyzes tumor tissue, metastatic biopsies, or blood containing tumor DNA to identify molecular markers and mutations in DNA repair genes
Relationships:
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: MUTATION
  - Description: Tumor testing looks for specific abnormalities and mutations in DNA repair genes
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
  - Target: TUMOR TESTING
  - Description: Tumor testing is one of the two basic types of genetic testing for prostate cancer care
  - Source: SOMATIC MUTATION
  - Target: TUMOR TESTING
  - Description: Tumor testing detects somatic mutations or abnormalities in tumor DNA and DNA repair genes
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: A biopsy of a tumor or metastasis provides the tissue sample used for tumor testing
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: METASTASIS
  - Description: Tumor testing can use a sample from a metastasis in lymph nodes, bones, liver, lung, or another affected area
  - Source: TUMOR TESTING
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Tumor testing helps determine the likelihood of cancer spread and aggressive transformation, which can inform tumor staging

**ENTITY: LYNCH SYNDROME**
Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes. It is a hereditary disorder that significantly increases the risk of developing several types of cancer, most notably colon cancer, as well as uterine (endometrial), ovarian, and other cancers. Lynch syndrome can also cause hereditary ovarian cancer and may increase the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. Because of its strong hereditary nature and elevated cancer risks, Lynch syndrome is recognized as a factor that may make germline genetic testing appropriate for affected individuals and their families.
Relationships:
  - Source: HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome can cause hereditary ovarian cancer
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Genetic testing can identify Lynch syndrome and other hereditary syndromes
  - Source: OVARIAN CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is a cause of ovarian cancer in addition to colon and uterine cancers
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: GERMLINE TESTING
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is listed as a factor making germline testing more appropriate
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the disease’s onset.
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: ADRENAL TUMORS
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is a hereditary risk factor for developing an adrenal tumor.
  - Source: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Target: GENETIC COUNSELING
  - Description: Lynch syndrome is a factor that should lead a person to ask about genetic counseling
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: If colon cancer is dMMR/MSI-H, mismatch repair testing leads to testing for Lynch syndrome

**ENTITY: DNA SEQUENCING**
Description: A biomarker test to look for mutations in TP53 and IGHV genes in CLL cells.
Relationships:
  - Source: CLL
  - Target: DNA SEQUENCING
  - Description: DNA sequencing is a biomarker test used for CLL cells.</data>
    </node>
