      <data key="d3">Fertility-sparing treatment is a therapeutic approach for cervical cancer that is confined to the cervix, specifically aimed at preserving the possibility of natural pregnancy. This option may be considered when the cancer is limited to the cervix, and it is particularly relevant for early-stage cervical cancer. One such surgical procedure is trachelectomy, which removes the cancerous tissue while retaining the uterus and part of the cervix to support future fertility. After undergoing fertility-sparing treatment, follow-up MRI is recommended to monitor for recurrence or complications. Overall, this treatment strategy balances oncologic safety with reproductive preservation for eligible patients.
Relationships:
  - Source: FERTILITY-SPARING SURGERY
  - Target: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Description: Fertility-sparing surgery is a type of fertility-sparing treatment
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Description: Fertility-sparing treatment involves surgery that does not remove the uterus or ovaries
  - Source: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Target: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Description: Fertility-sparing treatment is used for early-stage cervical cancer to allow preservation of fertility.
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Description: CERVICAL CANCER and FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT are directly related in certain clinical situations. Specifically, fertility-sparing treatment may be an option for individuals diagnosed with cervical cancer that is confined to the cervix, meaning the cancer has not spread beyond the cervical region. This applies when the disease is limited to the cervix itself, and such an approach may be considered as a treatment possibility in these early, localized cases.
  - Source: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Target: PREGNANCY
  - Description: Fertility-sparing treatment may allow future pregnancy
  - Source: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Target: TRACHELECTOMY
  - Description: Trachelectomy is a fertility-sparing surgery
  - Source: MRI
  - Target: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Description: MRI of the pelvis is recommended 6 months after fertility-sparing surgery and yearly for 2 to 3 years.
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Description: Cone biopsy can be used as part of a fertility-sparing treatment approach
  - Source: RADICAL TRACHELECTOMY
  - Target: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Description: Radical trachelectomy is a fertility-sparing surgical option</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
