    <node id="PREGNANCY">
      <data key="d0">PREGNANCY</data>
      <data key="d1">PREGNANCY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">PREGNANCY is a biological process in which progesterone plays a role, defined as the condition that occurs when an egg is fertilized and a fetus grows and develops in the uterus. It is the state of carrying an unborn baby, and it is a condition/event that can be significantly affected by cancer treatment decisions. Natural pregnancy may be impacted by cancer treatments, and this topic is often discussed alongside fertility preservation options. The condition is also considered in screening and testing discussions, and an obstetrician is the specialist who manages pregnancy. Planning a pregnancy both during and after cancer treatment requires careful discussion with a doctor, as treatment may affect fertility. However, fertility-sparing treatment may allow for the possibility of pregnancy in the future.
Relationships:
  - Source: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Target: PREGNANCY
  - Description: Fertility-sparing treatment may allow future pregnancy
  - Source: NATURAL PREGNANCY
  - Target: PREGNANCY
  - Description: Natural pregnancy is a type of pregnancy</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
