    <node id="RELATIONSHIP: IMAGING → BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">RELATIONSHIP: IMAGING → BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIP: IMAGING → BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">IMAGING and BIOPSY are complementary procedures in the management of glioma and other cancers, though the order in which they are used depends on the clinical context. In cases where a biopsy has already confirmed disease progression, IMAGING is the next step to determine whether the cancer is locally progressive or has become metastatic. This helps guide further treatment by clarifying the extent and spread of the disease. Conversely, when initial IMAGING findings suggest the presence of a glioma, a BIOPSY is then performed as the next step to confirm the diagnosis. In this scenario, imaging serves as the screening or detection tool that prompts tissue sampling for pathological confirmation. Thus, the relationship between IMAGING and BIOPSY is bidirectional: imaging can lead to biopsy when a suspicious lesion is found, and biopsy can lead to imaging when progression is confirmed, each serving distinct roles in diagnosis and disease monitoring. Together, they form an integrated diagnostic and surveillance pathway for brain tumors and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RELATIONSHIP: IMAGING → BIOPSY
Description: IMAGING and BIOPSY are complementary procedures in the management of glioma and other cancers, though the order in which they are used depends on the clinical context. In cases where a biopsy has already confirmed disease progression, IMAGING is the next step to determine whether the cancer is locally progressive or has become metastatic. This helps guide further treatment by clarifying the extent and spread of the disease. Conversely, when initial IMAGING findings suggest the presence of a glioma, a BIOPSY is then performed as the next step to confirm the diagnosis. In this scenario, imaging serves as the screening or detection tool that prompts tissue sampling for pathological confirmation. Thus, the relationship between IMAGING and BIOPSY is bidirectional: imaging can lead to biopsy when a suspicious lesion is found, and biopsy can lead to imaging when progression is confirmed, each serving distinct roles in diagnosis and disease monitoring. Together, they form an integrated diagnostic and surveillance pathway for brain tumors and other cancers.</data>
    </node>
