      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
Description: Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, cervix, and surrounding supportive tissues. Specifically, the operation removes the uterus, cervix, much of the connective tissue holding the cervix, and the top quarter to third of the vagina. It is performed through an abdominal incision. In addition to these structures, radical hysterectomy typically includes pelvic lymph node evaluation or dissection, which is important for staging and treating cervical cancer. The procedure is most commonly used for cervical cancer, and it may also be considered for recurrent cervical cancer, sometimes in combination with brachytherapy in carefully selected patients. This comprehensive approach aims to remove cancerous tissue and assess whether the cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes.
Relationships:
  - Source: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy and pelvic exenteration are both surgical treatments for recurrent cervical cancer; pelvic exenteration is more extensive.
