    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="UNITED STATES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">UNITED STATES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The text describes U.S. statistics showing that Black males are more likely to develop, be diagnosed at an advanced stage, and die from prostate cancer compared with white males.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="RESEARCHERS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">RESEARCHERS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Researchers are trying to understand the reasons for prostate cancer disparities and improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Prostate cancer is a disease that can be grouped into categories based on its progression. One such category is advanced-stage prostate cancer. Advanced-stage prostate cancer is a stage of prostate cancer in which the cancer has spread outside the prostate gland. In this context, advanced-stage prostate cancer is defined as a distinct category or stage within the broader classification of prostate cancer, representing the more severe end of the disease spectrum where the malignancy has metastasized beyond the organ of origin.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Health care professionals describe prostate cancer aggressiveness, recommend screening, and treat the disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Prostate cancer is a disease that originates in the prostate gland. It can be categorized into different stages based on how far it has progressed. Early-stage prostate cancer is one such category or stage of prostate cancer. This stage is defined by the fact that the cancer has not spread beyond the prostate gland. In other words, early-stage prostate cancer remains localized within the prostate, with no evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues or distant metastasis. As a subset of prostate cancer, early-stage prostate cancer represents a less advanced form of the disease, often associated with a better prognosis and more treatment options than later stages. Thus, while prostate cancer encompasses all stages of the disease, early-stage prostate cancer specifically refers to the confined, localized phase.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy samples are examined to determine whether prostate cancer is present.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">ACTIVE SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Active surveillance is a management option used for prostate cancer, especially early-stage disease, to avoid immediate treatment</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Prostate cancer can advance by metastasis, a process defined as the spread of cancer cells outside the prostate to other organs and tissues. This form of progression is characteristic of advanced-stage prostate cancer. Metastasis occurs when cancer cells travel through the bloodstream or the lymphatic system, enabling the cancer to disseminate beyond the prostate to distant sites in the body. In this way, prostate cancer metastasis involves the movement of malignant cells from the prostate via circulatory or lymphatic routes, ultimately establishing secondary growths in other organs and tissues.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="GLEASON SCORE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">GLEASON SCORE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Gleason score estimates how aggressive prostate cancer is and whether it will spread.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROSTATE CANCER" target="PSA DENSITY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA DENSITY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Higher PSA density indicates a greater likelihood of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
