    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="HEALTH CHECKUP" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CHECKUP</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Health checkups can include breast cancer screening and discussion of breast health</data>
      <data key="d12">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="CARCINOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">CARCINOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Almost all breast cancers are carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d12">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="METASTATIC BREAST CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTATIC BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Metastatic breast cancer is advanced breast cancer that has spread beyond the breast and nearby lymph nodes. It is a form of breast cancer characterized by the movement of cancer cells from the original tumor site to other parts of the body. Breast cancer, in its earlier stages, is typically confined to the breast tissue and possibly nearby lymph nodes; however, when it becomes metastatic, the disease has progressed to a more advanced stage. Thus, metastatic breast cancer represents the most severe form of breast cancer, in which the cancer has escaped the local region and established itself in distant organs or tissues.</data>
      <data key="d12">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
