      <data key="d12">ABDOMINAL CRAMPING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team talks with the patient about the types of chemotherapy, doses, timing, and side effects</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy and immunotherapy are both systemic cancer treatments, meaning they travel through the bloodstream to affect cells throughout the body. They are commonly used together or as separate options depending on the cancer type and its molecular characteristics. For instance, in certain cancers, chemotherapy is recommended for tumors that are pMMR/MSS (mismatch repair-proficient/microsatellite stable), while immunotherapy is preferred for tumors that are dMMR/MSI-H (mismatch repair-deficient/microsatellite instability-high). Both treatments are also standard systemic therapy options for bladder cancer. In the context of metastatic anal cancer, immunotherapy is specifically recommended when the disease does not respond to, or worsens after, chemotherapy. Despite their different mechanismschemotherapy directly targets rapidly dividing cells, while immunotherapy harnesses the immune systemthe two approaches share several similar side effects, including nausea, fever, infection, diarrhea, and fatigue. Overall, chemotherapy and immunotherapy play distinct but complementary roles in cancer care, with the choice of treatment guided by tumor biology, cancer type, and response to prior therapies.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="TARGETED THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">TARGETED THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Targeted therapy can be combined with chemotherapy, and may have fewer side effects than chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d12">TARGETED THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="FOLFCIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">FOLFCIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FOLFCIS is a chemotherapy regimen option for metastatic anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="DCF" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">DCF</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">DCF is a chemotherapy regimen option for metastatic anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">DCF</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Supportive care is a specialized area of healthcare focused on relieving the side effects and symptoms caused by cancer treatment, particularly chemotherapy. It is used to manage the physical and emotional challenges that may arise during treatment, helping patients maintain their quality of life. While chemotherapy is the primary treatment aimed at targeting cancer cells, supportive care works alongside it to address the adverse effects it can cause, such as nausea, fatigue, pain, and other treatment-related complications. In this way, supportive care plays a crucial complementary role in the overall management of cancer patients, ensuring that the side effects of chemotherapy are minimized and that patients are better able to tolerate and continue their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is a form of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="FERTILITY PRESERVATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy can have a detrimental effect on fertility, as the treatment may harm reproductive organs or function. Because of this potential impact, patients who are scheduled to undergo chemotherapy may need to consider fertility preservation options before starting their treatment. Fertility preservation, therefore, becomes an important proactive step for individuals who wish to protect their ability to have biological children in the future after completing chemotherapy. This process should ideally be addressed prior to the initiation of chemotherapy, as the treatment itself can compromise fertility, making advance planning essential.</data>
      <data key="d12">FERTILITY PRESERVATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy is one of the main treatments for ovarian cancer. In cases where ovarian cancer has spread too extensively for initial surgery, chemotherapy is needed first.</data>
      <data key="d12">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
