    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging is often performed before and after surgery, with surgery enabling more accurate pathologic staging.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgery and cancer treatment are closely interconnected, with surgery serving as a major therapeutic approach in oncology. Surgery is a form of cancer treatment that is used to remove cancerous tissue from the body. It is also one component of a broader cancer treatment plan, which may include other modalities such as radiation therapy, chemotherapy, or immunotherapy. In the context of head and neck cancers specifically, surgery is considered a complex form of cancer treatment due to the intricate anatomy of the region and the need for precise removal of tumors while preserving critical functions like breathing, swallowing, and speech. Additionally, discussions of surgery as a cancer treatment include attention to surgery side effects in cancer care, acknowledging that the procedure can have physical and functional impacts that must be managed before, during, and after treatment. Thus, surgery is both a distinct, direct method for excising cancer and an integral part of multidisciplinary cancer care, with its own risks and benefits that are weighed carefully by the medical team.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">SURGERY and CLINICAL TRIALS are both recognized treatment approaches in oncology, applicable to different cancer types. According to the provided descriptions, surgery is a treatment option for thyroid cancer, and clinical trials are also listed as another treatment option for the same disease. Similarly, both surgery and clinical trials are identified as treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer. Thus, these two entities are consistently presented as parallel therapeutic avenues for both thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. No contradictions exist between the descriptions. The combined understanding is that SURGERY and CLINICAL TRIALS are each considered viable treatment modalities for thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer, with clinical trials offering access to investigational or alternative approaches alongside standard surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="PDEMA/MOHS PROCEDURE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">PDEMA/MOHS PROCEDURE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PDEMA/Mohs procedure is a surgical procedure performed to remove skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">PDEMA/MOHS PROCEDURE</data>
    </edge>
