      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">SMOKING OR VAPING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Smoking and vaping can limit wound healing and greatly increase side effects during and after surgery</data>
      <data key="d12">SMOKING OR VAPING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="SMOKING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">SMOKING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Smoking greatly increases the chance of side effects during and after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURGERY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Fertility-sparing treatment involves surgery that does not remove the uterus or ovaries</data>
      <data key="d12">FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgery is a primary and often initial treatment for ovarian cancer. It serves two key purposes: removing the cancerous tissue and determining the extent of the disease, a process known as staging. By surgically removing ovarian cancer, doctors aim to eliminate as much of the tumor as possible, which is essential for effective treatment. Additionally, the information gathered during surgery helps assess how far the cancer has spread, guiding subsequent treatment decisions. Thus, surgery plays a dual role in both treating and evaluating ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
