      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging is used to reflect prognosis and guide treatment decisions for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biomarker testing is part of the information gathered during staging to identify gene mutations or proteins that may guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging determines when chemoradiation is recommended, such as for high-grade stage I, stage II with sphincter invasion, and stage III cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="LOCAL EXCISION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging determines whether local excision is an option, since it is recommended only for certain stage I and IIA cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created and developed the cancer staging system described in the text, which is the staging system used for cancer staging. This AJCC staging system is one of the two standard ways to describe the extent of ovarian cancer. As the entity responsible for this widely used framework, the American Joint Committee on Cancer established a systematic method for categorizing the progression and spread of cancer, providing clinicians and researchers with a common language to describe tumor characteristics. The cancer staging system developed by the committee is integral to treatment planning and prognosis, and its application to ovarian cancer represents one of its key clinical uses, where it serves as a standard alongside another classification approach to define how far the disease has advanced.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics developed the FIGO staging system, which is the most commonly used system for describing ovarian cancer extent.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER STAGING" target="NCCN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN provides patient education that explains cancer staging, including TNM scores and numbered stages.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM SCORING" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TNM SCORING</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">TNM scores are assigned as part of cancer staging and are then combined to assign a numbered stage.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL STAGING" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical staging is the pre-treatment portion of cancer staging. Cancer staging is often performed as clinical staging before treatment begins.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL STAGING" target="BREAST EXAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST EXAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The breast exam is part of the physical exam that contributes to clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d12">BREAST EXAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created and developed the cancer staging system described in the text, which is the staging system used for cancer staging. This AJCC staging system is one of the two standard ways to describe the extent of ovarian cancer. As the entity responsible for this widely used framework, the American Joint Committee on Cancer established a systematic method for categorizing the progression and spread of cancer, providing clinicians and researchers with a common language to describe tumor characteristics. The cancer staging system developed by the committee is integral to treatment planning and prognosis, and its application to ovarian cancer represents one of its key clinical uses, where it serves as a standard alongside another classification approach to define how far the disease has advanced.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="NPC STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">NPC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The AJCC staging system is used in NPC staging.</data>
