      <data key="d7">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d8">TUMOR STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Tumor staging uses the M category to indicate whether cancer has metastasized outside the pelvis</data>
      <data key="d12">TUMOR STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The presence or absence of metastasis is a key factor in determining cancer stage and is represented in the M score.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="FOLFCIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d8">FOLFCIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">FOLFCIS is used to treat metastatic anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">FOLFCIS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="DCF" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d8">DCF</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">DCF is used to treat metastatic anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">DCF</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="METASTATIC PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTATIC PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Metastatic prostate cancer has spread through metastasis; the text uses the terms interchangeably</data>
      <data key="d12">METASTATIC PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Prostate cancer can spread to other areas of the body through metastasis, and this spread is called advanced-stage disease</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="METASTASIS" target="PROGRESSION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d8">PROGRESSION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Metastatic cancer may progress during or after chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d12">PROGRESSION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="TUMOR STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">TUMOR STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging and scans provide information used in tumor staging for prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">TUMOR STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Care teams rely on imaging tests to determine if cancer is present, has spread, or has responded to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">IMAGING encompasses the various imaging tests used in cancer evaluation, including X-ray, CT, MRI, PET, PET-CT, ultrasound, mammography, and bone scans.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="RADIOLOGIST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging is interpreted by a radiologist who writes an imaging report and sends results to the patient's health care provider.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">IMAGING and BIOPSY are complementary procedures in the management of glioma and other cancers, though the order in which they are used depends on the clinical context.

In cases where a biopsy has already confirmed disease progression, IMAGING is the next step to determine whether the cancer is locally progressive or has become metastatic. This helps guide further treatment by clarifying the extent and spread of the disease.

Conversely, when initial IMAGING findings suggest the presence of a glioma, a BIOPSY is then performed as the next step to confirm the diagnosis. In this scenario, imaging serves as the screening or detection tool that prompts tissue sampling for pathological confirmation.

Thus, the relationship between IMAGING and BIOPSY is bidirectional: imaging can lead to biopsy when a suspicious lesion is found, and biopsy can lead to imaging when progression is confirmed, each serving distinct roles in diagnosis and disease monitoring. Together, they form an integrated diagnostic and surveillance pathway for brain tumors and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging, specifically computed tomography (CT), serves as an initial imaging modality and is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging. Additionally, CT with contrast is an imaging option for follow-up of cervical cancer stages 2, 3, and 4A.</data>
      <data key="d12">CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is an initial imaging option for cervical cancer and is used for post-treatment follow-up and surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PET" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PET</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET is an initial imaging option for cervical cancer and can be combined with CT or MRI for more detailed diagnostic information.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ultrasound is a type of imaging modality used to visualize internal structures of the body. As an imaging technique, it falls under the broader category of diagnostic imaging, which encompasses various methods for producing images of the human body for clinical analysis and medical intervention. Specifically, ultrasound is classified as a type of imaging test, meaning it is a diagnostic procedure that utilizes high-frequency sound waves to generate real-time images of organs, tissues, and blood flow, without the use of ionizing radiation. In the context of the relationships between these entities, IMAGING serves as the overarching category, while ULTRASOUND is a specific subset or application within that category. Thus, ultrasound is both a form of imaging and a distinct imaging test used in medical practice.</data>
      <data key="d12">ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="SURVEILLANCE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surveillance is the regular follow-up after cancer treatment to detect recurrence as early as possible, and imaging is a key component of surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="RECURRENCE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging is used to detect recurrence, which is the return of cancer after a cancer-free period.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging encompasses various diagnostic techniques, including the CT scan (computed tomography scan). A CT scan is an imaging test that combines multiple x-ray images to create a detailed three-dimensional picture of internal structures. In clinical practice, CT is one of the imaging tests used in surveillance after recurrence treatment, allowing healthcare providers to monitor patients for signs of recurrence and assess response to therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="OPEN MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">OPEN MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Open MRI is a less confining type of MRI useful for patients with claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CLINICAL STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging provides essential information for clinical staging, assessing tumor spread, and planning treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CONTRAST MATERIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CONTRAST MATERIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Some imaging tests require contrast material, and patients should inform doctors about past allergic reactions to contrast before imaging with CT or MRI.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MRI TECHNOLOGIST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI TECHNOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">During MRI, the technologist operates the scanner and checks for metal in the body.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging tests and scans are used to find prostate cancer and determine whether it has spread</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="COLON CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT with contrast is the main imaging test used to determine the extent of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">In primary CNS lymphoma, MRI or CT scans are used to detect tumors and aid diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BASAL CELL SKIN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BASAL CELL SKIN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">In some cases, imaging is also used to evaluate basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">For cervical cancer, post-treatment imaging may include CT, MRI, and/or PET/CT scans, typically from the neck to the midthigh, if recurrence or spread is suspected.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="ANAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">ANAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">For stage II and III anal cancers in complete remission, radiologic imaging such as CT or MRI may be recommended every year for three years to check for spread.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="RECTAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">For rectal cancer after endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) or transanal local excision, surveillance imaging with endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI with contrast is recommended to check for recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MEDICAL BINDER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL BINDER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging results are often kept in a medical binder, and patients are advised to obtain copies of their imaging results for their records.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PATIENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The patient should obtain copies of imaging results and keep them organized.</data>
      <data key="d12">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Positron emission tomography (PET) is a type of imaging technology and a specific imaging test that falls under the broader category of imaging. It is a medical diagnostic procedure that relies on radiotracersradioactive substances introduced into the bodyto detect and visualize cancer activity. By tracking how these radiotracers behave, PET imaging provides detailed information about metabolic and physiological processes, making it particularly useful for identifying malignant tumors, evaluating how far cancer has spread, and monitoring the effectiveness of cancer treatments. The entity "imaging" serves as the overarching field, while "positron emission tomography" is a specialized modality within that field, distinguished by its use of radiotracers to target and highlight areas of abnormal cellular activity, especially in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d12">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">IMAGING encompasses various techniques used to visualize internal structures and detect disease. Among these, PET/CT is a more advanced imaging test that may be needed to detect cancer spread farther than lymph nodes. PET/CT is an imaging test that combines positron emission tomography (PET) with computed tomography (CT), allowing for both functional and anatomical assessment in a single procedure.</data>
      <data key="d12">PET/CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Positron emission tomography (PET) is a type of imaging technology and a specific imaging test that falls under the broader category of imaging. It is a medical diagnostic procedure that relies on radiotracersradioactive substances introduced into the bodyto detect and visualize cancer activity. By tracking how these radiotracers behave, PET imaging provides detailed information about metabolic and physiological processes, making it particularly useful for identifying malignant tumors, evaluating how far cancer has spread, and monitoring the effectiveness of cancer treatments. The entity "imaging" serves as the overarching field, while "positron emission tomography" is a specialized modality within that field, distinguished by its use of radiotracers to target and highlight areas of abnormal cellular activity, especially in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (CT) SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (CT) SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A CT scan of lymph nodes is recommended imaging after biopsy confirms cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d12">COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY (CT) SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PSA TEST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PSA TEST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A PSA test is often paired with imaging to get a more complete picture of prostate health</data>
      <data key="d12">PSA TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="RADIOLOGY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIOLOGY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging studies are performed in the field of radiology.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), also referred to simply as IMAGING in certain contexts, is a type of imaging that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed pictures of the body's internal structures. As an imaging test, MRI provides highly detailed images without the use of ionizing radiation, making it a valuable diagnostic tool.</data>
      <data key="d12">MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT OR MRI OF ABDOMEN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CT OR MRI OF ABDOMEN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT or MRI of the abdomen is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d12">CT OR MRI OF ABDOMEN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMORADIATION" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging determines when chemoradiation is recommended, such as for high-grade stage I, stage II with sphincter invasion, and stage III cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics developed the FIGO staging system, which is the most commonly used system for describing ovarian cancer extent.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LOCAL EXCISION" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">LOCAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging determines whether local excision is an option, since it is recommended only for certain stage I and IIA cancers.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LIVER FUNCTION TESTS" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">LIVER FUNCTION TESTS</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team monitors liver health using LFTs.</data>
      <data key="d12">LIVER FUNCTION TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Screening is needed because some cancer treatments can reactivate hepatitis B or hepatitis C and cause liver damage.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The hepatitis blood test tells the treatment team whether the patient has or had hepatitis B or C.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEPATITIS B AND HEPATITIS C SCREENING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) TEST" target="BLOOD TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) TEST</data>
      <data key="d8">BLOOD TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The AFP test is a type of blood test.</data>
      <data key="d12">ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) TEST" target="PRIMARY LIVER CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) TEST</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY LIVER CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Elevated or rising AFP may be a sign of primary liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT TEAM" target="DOCTOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor is part of the patient's treatment team.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER CARE TEAM" target="RICHTER TRANSFORMATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">RICHTER TRANSFORMATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The cancer care team tells the patient if they have Richter transformation and directs them to read Chapter 5.</data>
      <data key="d12">RICHTER TRANSFORMATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER CARE TEAM" target="FERTILITY SPECIALIST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY SPECIALIST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The cancer care team may give the patient a referral to a fertility specialist to discuss fertility preservation.</data>
      <data key="d12">FERTILITY SPECIALIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CANCER CARE TEAM" target="FERTILITY COUNSELOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CANCER CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY COUNSELOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The cancer care team advises the patient to talk with a fertility counselor before starting treatment to understand fertility preservation options.</data>
      <data key="d12">FERTILITY COUNSELOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="QUESTIONS TO ASK" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">QUESTIONS TO ASK</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The suggested questions are directed to the patient's care team</data>
      <data key="d12">QUESTIONS TO ASK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="QUESTIONS TO ASK" target="PATIENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">QUESTIONS TO ASK</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The Questions to Ask section helps patients ask their care team questions</data>
      <data key="d12">PATIENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team plays a key role in evaluating whether a clinical trial is appropriate for a patient. They can be consulted directly to determine if a clinical trial might be right for the individual. To make this determination, the care team uses the patient's performance status as a key factor in assessing eligibility for a clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="TREATMENT TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical trials are research studies that evaluate medical interventions in human participants. Treatment trials are a specific type of clinical trial, meaning that all treatment trials fall under the broader category of clinical trials. In the context of cancer, treatment trials focus specifically on cancer treatmentstesting new therapies, drug combinations, surgical techniques, or other interventions to determine their safety and effectiveness. Because most cancer clinical trials are treatment trials, treatment trials constitute a major subset of clinical research in oncology. Thus, while every treatment trial is a clinical trial, not every clinical trial is a treatment trial; clinical trials also include prevention, screening, diagnostic, and quality-of-life studies. In summary, treatment trials are a type of clinical trial that centers on cancer treatments and represents the majority of cancer-related clinical research.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NCCN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN Cancer Centers and NCCN patient guidelines provide information about cancer clinical trials and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="SURGERY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Enrolling in a clinical trial is another treatment option alongside surgery for eligible patients.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A clinical trial is a treatment option that may be considered for a patient. Clinical trials offer potential new therapies and serve as another treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="UNITED STATES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">UNITED STATES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The text describes finding clinical trials in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCI provides a portal for patients to search for cancer clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">U.S. NATIONAL LIBRARY OF MEDICINE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NLM maintains clinicaltrials.gov, where patients can search for clinical trials worldwide.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="NCCN CANCER CENTERS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN Cancer Centers serve as a key resource for locating clinical trials, specifically within the United States. These centers assist patients in identifying and accessing clinical trial opportunities, making them a valuable source of information for those seeking participation in research studies. The entity CLINICAL TRIAL is central to this resource, as the primary function of the NCCN Cancer Centers is to connect individuals with relevant clinical trials. In summary, NCCN Cancer Centers provide a United Statesbased resource dedicated to helping patients find clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN CANCER CENTERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 3 TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 3 TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Phase 3 trials are a phase of clinical trials comparing a new approach to a standard treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 2 TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 2 TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Phase 2 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying effectiveness against a specific type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 4 TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 4 TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Phase 4 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying long-term safety and benefit after FDA approval.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 1 TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 1 TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Phase 1 trials are a phase of clinical trials studying safety and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 3 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 3 CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A Phase 3 trial is a clinical trial phase that compares a new treatment to a standard treatment and may lead to FDA approval.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 4 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 4 CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A Phase 4 trial is a post-approval clinical trial phase that studies long-term safety and benefit.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="APPOINTMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">APPOINTMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A clinical trial may require extra appointments, resulting in indirect costs such as transportation or child care.</data>
      <data key="d12">APPOINTMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CLINICAL TRIAL" target="PHASE 1 CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d8">PHASE 1 CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A Phase 1 trial is an early phase in the clinical trial process, focused on dose, safety, and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team relies on MRI scans as a key diagnostic and monitoring tool throughout the patients journey, including during diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up care. Because the MRI procedure involves being inside an enclosed scanner, the care team should be informed if the patient feels nervous or anxious in enclosed spaces before the scan takes place. This allows the team to make appropriate accommodations and ensure the patients comfort and safety.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="GLIOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">GLIOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is the most useful imaging test for identifying a glioma.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BRAIN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">BRAIN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is used to visualize the brain and central nervous system, including for diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="SPINE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">SPINE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is used for spine imaging.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BREAST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is used for breast imaging, where images are interpreted by a breast diagnostic radiologist.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BLADDER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">BLADDER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI can evaluate the bladder and urinary tract to check for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="RECTAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">In rectal cancer, pelvic MRI shows how deeply the tumor has grown into the rectal wall and whether it has spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is used as an initial imaging test to determine the extent of disease before treatment, with and without contrast to examine the primary tumor and neck.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET can be combined with MRI for imaging.</data>
      <data key="d12">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI and ultrasound are imaging tests used in breast cancer evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d12">ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI of the pelvis is recommended 6 months after fertility-sparing surgery and yearly for 2 to 3 years.</data>
      <data key="d12">FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
