    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">ALL is the acronym for acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="T-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts. One of the two main types of ALL is T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). T-ALL specifically originates in T-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell that plays a central role in the immune system. As one of the two primary subtypes of ALL, T-ALL is distinguished from the other main type, which arises from B-cell lymphocytes. Together, these two subtypes encompass the majority of acute lymphoblastic leukemia cases. T-ALL develops when genetic mutations or other alterations cause T-cell precursors to multiply uncontrollably, leading to an accumulation of abnormal cells in the bone marrow, blood, and sometimes other organs. The disease is aggressive and requires prompt treatment, typically involving chemotherapy, and in some cases targeted therapy or stem cell transplantation. Although T-ALL and B-ALL share many clinical features, they differ in their cellular origins, immunophenotypic markers, and certain molecular characteristics, which can influence treatment response and prognosis. T-ALL is more common in adolescents and young adults, while B-ALL is more frequent in younger children. Because T-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, all general information about ALLincluding its causes, symptoms such as fatigue, fever, easy bruising, and bone pain, as well as diagnostic approaches like blood counts and bone marrow biopsyalso applies to T-ALL. However, specific management strategies for T-ALL may be adapted based on the distinct biology of T-cell disease. In summary, T-ALL is one of the two main forms of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, originating specifically from T-cell lymphocytes, and is a distinct yet closely related entity within the broader ALL classification.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" target="BURKITT LEUKEMIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Burkitt leukemia, also known as mature B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), is a rare subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia. It is a distinct form of the disease, categorized under the broader entity of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and is recognized for its mature B-cell phenotype.</data>
      <data key="d12">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
    </edge>
