      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">B-ALL (B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature B lymphocytes. BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a distinct subtype of B-ALL defined by a specific genetic abnormality: the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene, resulting from a translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (the Philadelphia chromosome). This genetic alteration drives the leukemia and has important implications for diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment. As a subtype of B-ALL, BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL shares the general features of B-ALL but is set apart by this molecular marker, making targeted therapies particularly relevant for this form of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-ALL" target="MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d8">MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Mature B-cell ALL, also called Burkitt leukemia, is a rare subtype of B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="APL" target="BPDCN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">APL</data>
      <data key="d8">BPDCN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">APL (acute promyelocytic leukemia) and BPDCN (blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm) are both recognized as subtypes of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These two entities are addressed together in the same NCCN patient guidelines, which provide comprehensive guidance for patients and caregivers on these specific AML subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d12">BPDCN</data>
    </edge>
