      <data key="d8">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Brachytherapy may also be given after pelvic exenteration or as part of recurrence treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">PELVIC EXENTERATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PELVIC EXENTERATION" target="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PELVIC EXENTERATION</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Systemic therapy is recommended after pelvic exenteration surgery for local/regional recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d12">PELVIC EXENTERATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PELVIC EXENTERATION" target="EBRT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PELVIC EXENTERATION</data>
      <data key="d8">EBRT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">After pelvic exenteration, external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is recommended along with systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">EBRT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="EXTRA IMAGING" target="IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">EXTRA IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Extra imaging is a form of imaging used before treatment to look for lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d12">EXTRA IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LYMPH NODE EVALUATION" target="PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
      <data key="d8">PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Pelvic lymph node evaluation is a type of lymph node evaluation</data>
      <data key="d12">PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that removes more connective tissue and the upper vagina.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">MODIFIED RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A hysterectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the uterus. Modified radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that, in addition to removing the uterus, also removes part of the surrounding connective tissue and a portion of the vagina. This distinguishes it from a standard hysterectomy, which typically removes only the uterus and possibly the cervix. The modified radical hysterectomy is thus a more extensive procedure than a simple hysterectomy, as it addresses additional tissue to achieve a wider margin of removal.</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="EXTRAFASCIAL HYSTERECTOMY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">EXTRAFASCIAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Extrafascial hysterectomy, also referred to as simple hysterectomy, is a surgical procedure and a specific type of hysterectomy. As a form of hysterectomy, it involves the surgical removal of the uterus. More precisely, the extrafascial approach removes only the uterus and the cervix. Thus, while it is categorized under the broader term "hysterectomy," it is distinguished by its limited scope of tissue removal, leaving other surrounding structures intact. This description incorporates both the general classification and the specific anatomical details of the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d12">EXTRAFASCIAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Hysterectomy is considered in cervical cancer treatment but is not performed if cancer has spread beyond the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Pelvic lymph nodes may be removed during hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d12">PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="SURGICAL MENOPAUSE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGICAL MENOPAUSE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgical menopause can be caused by hysterectomy that removes the ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">In cases where cervical cancer has spread beyond the cervix, a hysterectomy is not performed. Instead, pre-treatment surgical staging is considered to assess the extent of the disease. As part of this pre-treatment surgical staging, lymph nodes may be removed and tested to determine whether the cancer has spread to those nodes. This approach helps guide treatment decisions without proceeding to hysterectomy.</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="LYMPH NODE REMOVAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">LYMPH NODE REMOVAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">When cancer has spread beyond the cervix, a hysterectomy is not performed; instead, lymph nodes may be removed for staging.</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="MENOPAUSE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">MENOPAUSE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If hysterectomy removes both ovaries, it causes menopause.</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HYSTERECTOMY" target="OVARY PRESERVATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARY PRESERVATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The ovaries may or may not be removed during hysterectomy; ovary preservation keeps them intact.</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION" target="PELVIC LYMPH NODE DISSECTION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
      <data key="d8">PELVIC LYMPH NODE DISSECTION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Pelvic lymph node dissection is a surgical form of pelvic lymph node evaluation</data>
      <data key="d12">PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION" target="PARA-AORTIC LYMPH NODE REMOVAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION</data>
